.TH INTRO 2
.SH NAME
intro \- the system calls of the SIEOS kernel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"      /* mk/abi.h: numbers, types, errors */
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The kernel offers very little: processes and threads, memory, time,
message passing (IPC) and, for drivers, access to devices. Everything
else (the terminal, the disk, files, users) is done by ordinary programs,
"servers", that other programs talk to with messages
.RB ( ipc (7)).
These pages describe the calls; the servers' messages are in section 4.
.PP
A call is one instruction. On x86-64,
.B SYSCALL
with the number (SYS_...) in rax and the arguments in rdi, rsi, rdx,
r10, r8, r9; the result comes back in rax (the instruction itself
overwrites rcx and r11). On arm64,
.B svc #0
with the number in x8 and the arguments in x0 to x5; the result comes
back in x0, every other register kept. mk.h wraps them:
.BR syscall6 ,
.BR syscall4 ,
and one inline function per call. Any pointer from a program is checked
by the kernel: a bad one gives
.BR EFAULT ,
never a crash.
.SH RETURN VALUE
A result >= 0 is success; a failure is a negative error number, -E...
(the POSIX numbers): there is no
.IR errno .
.SH ERRORS
.nf
EPERM    1  not allowed        EFAULT  14  bad address
ENOENT   2  no such entry      EBUSY   16  busy
ESRCH    3  no such process    EEXIST  17  already exists
EINTR    4  interrupted        EXDEV   18  not across volumes
EIO      5  input/output       ENOTDIR 20  not a directory
EBADF    9  bad handle         EISDIR  21  is a directory
ECHILD  10  no child           EINVAL  22  invalid argument
EAGAIN  11  try again          EMFILE  24  too many open
ENOMEM  12  out of memory      ENOSPC  28  no space left
EACCES  13  permission denied  EROFS   30  read only
EPIPE   32  the other end ended
ENAMETOOLONG 36  name too long
ENOSYS  38  not on this machine
ENOTEMPTY 39  directory not empty
ELOOP   40  too many symbolic links
ETIMEDOUT 110  timed out
.fi
.SH MESSAGES
.nf
typedef struct {
    uint64_t w[4];               /* w[0]: operation, or result */
    const void *sbuf; uint64_t slen;
    void *rbuf;       uint64_t rlen;
    int32_t pid, uid, gid, _pad; /* the sender, set by the kernel */
} msg_t;
.fi
.PP
Small values travel in
.IR w[] ;
larger data is copied by the kernel straight from the sender's
.I sbuf
into the receiver's
.I rbuf
(one copy, no buffering in the kernel).
.SH THE CALLS
.nf
Messages:  port_create  port_lookup  ipc_call  ipc_recv  ipc_reply
           ipc_reply_recv
Processes: sys_spawn  sys_exit  sys_wait  sys_ident  sys_tasks
           sys_info  sys_child_port  sys_modules  sys_want  sys_wanted
Threads:   thread_start (SYS_THREAD_CREATE, SYS_THREAD_EXIT)
           sys_thread_self  set_fs  sys_yield  sys_place
Signals:   kill  tkill  signal (SYS_SIGNAL, SYS_SIGRETURN)  sigwait
Memory:    sys_brk  sys_mmap  sys_munmap  sys_mprotect  sys_shm_create
           sys_shm_map  sys_shm_hold  sys_shm_close
Waiting:   sys_futex  sys_note_word  sys_note  sys_sleep  sys_wake
Time:      sys_clock  sys_time
Machine:   sys_power  sys_random  sys_debug  sys_log  sys_screen
Drivers:   irq_bind  irq_ack  irq_msi  map_phys  dma_alloc  dma_free
           pci_read (SYS_PCI)  sys_fdt  sys_acpi
.fi
.PP
Who may call what: anyone, unless the page says root (uid 0), the
supervisor
.RB ( init (8),
the first boot module), or drivers (processes the supervisor started
with
.BR SPAWN_DRIVER ).
An unknown number gives
.BR ENOSYS .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ipc (7),
.BR security (7),
.BR intro (4),
.BR sys_spawn (2),
.BR ipc_call (2)
